WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.988
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:17.688 --> 00:01:19.182
All right, hands in.

00:01:19.182 --> 00:01:20.730
On three, MIT.

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:23.620
One, two, three, MIT.

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:55.080
5...4...3...2...1

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:56.200
Past the barrier

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:22.760
Door is...

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:23.920
...wide open

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:38.320
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:41.801 --> 00:03:44.070
Hey guys, should we
climb some stairs?

00:03:44.070 --> 00:03:45.097
Let's climb some stairs.

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:16.140
We're done.

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:17.500
Oh yeah.

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:20.470
[APPLAUSE]

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:31.807
So we just survived our
week experience here

00:04:31.807 --> 00:04:33.140
at the DARPA Robotics Challenge.

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:36.490
MIT I think has
come in sixth place.

00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:38.420
After being about an
inch away from winning

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:42.040
the whole competition, our drill
slipped away from the wall.

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:44.170
Ended up being a task
we couldn't complete

00:04:44.170 --> 00:04:45.890
and that cost us
the whole challenge.

00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:48.030
We lost or first place spot.

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:49.720
We were on a first place run.

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:54.490
But I think we got to show the
world that a robot based very

00:04:54.490 --> 00:04:57.940
much on autonomy was capable
of competing with the very

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:00.780
best robots in the whole world.

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:03.100
We were using all kinds of
teleoperation and humans

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:03.930
in the loop.

00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:05.950
There were a few defining
moments this week.

00:05:05.950 --> 00:05:10.740
On our first run, we made just
as a simple operator error

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:12.887
where we forgot to turn
off the driving controller

00:05:12.887 --> 00:05:14.470
before we tried to
get out of the car.

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:22.110
We fell down, broke
our right arm.

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:25.950
But even though we planned to do
the entire course right-handed,

00:05:25.950 --> 00:05:27.990
the algorithms and
the operators were

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:31.870
able to recover and do basically
the entire course left-handed

00:05:31.870 --> 00:05:34.980
doing a seven point run with
eight points total in a very

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:36.180
respectable time.

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:37.336
It was awesome.

00:05:37.336 --> 00:05:38.050
Awesome.

00:05:38.050 --> 00:05:39.915
I couldn't be
prouder of the team.

00:05:39.915 --> 00:05:44.175
The way they handled the
stress, the drama, the pressure.

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:49.440
The way they used
their brains to get out

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:51.552
of every inconceivable
situation.

00:05:51.552 --> 00:05:53.510
I don't think there's
another group around that

00:05:53.510 --> 00:05:55.820
could have solved all
problems we solved this week.

00:05:55.820 --> 00:05:58.210
I mean, unbelievable debugging.

00:05:58.210 --> 00:06:03.160
I mean, it's impressive. so
I'm really proud of them.

00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:04.470
That close man.

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:06.470
That inch.

